<article-title>Skyline: an open source document editor for creating and analyzing targeted proteomics experiments</article-title>. <source>Bioinformatics</source>
<volume>26</volume>, <fpage>966</fpage>–<lpage>8</lpage> (<year>2010</year>).<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">20147306</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi" assigning-authority="pmc">10.1093/bioinformatics/btq054</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmcid">PMC2844992</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Fig. 1.</label><caption><title>Schematic illustration of the mechanism of sustained signaling through the formation of endosomal class B GPCR–G protein–βarr megacomplexes.</title><p id="P75">Binding of β-arrestin (βarr) to a GRK-phosphorylated GPCR tail (leaving the receptor intracellular core open) and subsequent receptor internalization allows for further G protein binding, forming a megaplex (black box). The megaplex continues to activate G protein, leading to sustained endosomal cAMP generation.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="nihms-1541120-f0001.jpg"><?image-name nihms-1541120-f0001.jpg?><?image-size 319530?><?image-md5 fef30e28c03bdbc1baf5c0a7479b95f0?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2098?><?image-original-width 3192?><?image-scaled-height 524?><?image-scaled-width 798?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/fef30e28c03b/nihms-1541120-f0001.jpg?><?thumb-name nihms-1541120-f0001.gif?><?thumb-size 3017?><?thumb-md5 e20940d41754c04c6d9a96aca63f0b12?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 65?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/e20940d41754/nihms-1541120-f0001.gif?></graphic></fig><fig id="F2" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Fig. 2.</label><caption><title>Cryo-EM structure of a β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–Gs–βarr1 megaplex.</title><p id="P76"><bold>a</bold>, Composite cryo-EM density map of the megaplex reconstruction fitted into a transparent envelope of the megaplex consensus structure. <bold>b</bold>, Same as in <bold>a</bold>, but with protein stabilizers (Nb35, Nb32, and Fab30) and consensus reconstruction removed. Red dashed lines indicate the flexible C-terminal tail connecting βarr1 to the receptor.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="nihms-1541120-f0002.jpg"><?image-name nihms-1541120-f0002.jpg?><?image-size 372942?><?image-md5 f34bbed492c906d18766bb8bf552af0e?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 2746?><?image-original-width 3520?><?image-scaled-height 624?><?image-scaled-width 800?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/f34bbed492c9/nihms-1541120-f0002.jpg?><?thumb-name nihms-1541120-f0002.gif?><?thumb-size 3395?><?thumb-md5 bd0f6e55d536d06c69029f1864b1c3d3?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 78?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/bd0f6e55d536/nihms-1541120-f0002.gif?></graphic></fig><fig id="F3" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Fig. 3.</label><caption><title>Structure and interactions of the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–Gs portion of the megaplex</title><p id="P77"><bold>a</bold>, Orthogonal views of the density map and model for the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–Gs portion of the megaplex. Small top image orients the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–Gs subcomplex in relation to the megaplex consensus structure. <bold>b</bold>, Intracellular view of the superimposition between the megaplex β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R and the inactive, carazolol-bound β<sub>2</sub>AR (PDB: 2RH1). Blue arrows indicate movements in transmembrane helices (TMs) 3,5,6 and 7 and their magnitudes. <bold>c</bold>, Intracellular view of the superimposition between the megaplex β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R and the Gs-bound β<sub>2</sub>AR (PDB: 3SN6). <bold>d</bold>, Ligand-binding pocket of the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R, with density for the agonist BI-167107 (BI-167107, green) in mesh. <bold>e</bold>, Interaction of the α5-helix of Gαs with hydrophobic residues on TM5 and TM6, and with polar residues between TM3 and TM5 of the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R. β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R residues are labeled in black for <bold>d</bold> and <bold>e</bold>. <bold>f</bold>, Structural comparison of the β<sub>2</sub>AR–Gs complex (pink, PDB: 3SN6) against the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–Gs portion of the megaplex, both aligned by their receptors. Curved arrows indicate 3° rotation of Gαs around an axis parallel to the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R-α5 helix contact and 3.4° rotation of Gβγ around an axis parallel to the plasma membrane seen in the megaplex.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="nihms-1541120-f0003.jpg"><?image-name nihms-1541120-f0003.jpg?><?image-size 524425?><?image-md5 2122988ee4547369d970e5219599a5cc?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 3426?><?image-original-width 4200?><?image-scaled-height 652?><?image-scaled-width 800?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/2122988ee454/nihms-1541120-f0003.jpg?><?thumb-name nihms-1541120-f0003.gif?><?thumb-size 3945?><?thumb-md5 b3c48d90c4d97d5d52a7a17032e42365?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 81?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/b3c48d90c4d9/nihms-1541120-f0003.gif?></graphic></fig><fig id="F4" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Fig. 4.</label><caption><title>Structure and interactions of the βarr1–V<sub>2</sub>T portion of the megaplex.</title><p id="P78"><bold>a</bold>, Density map and model for the megaplex βarr1–V<sub>2</sub>T. Mesh delineates density for the V<sub>2</sub>T. Small top image orients the βarr1–V<sub>2</sub>T subcomplex in relation to the megaplex consensus structure. <bold>b</bold>,<bold>c</bold>, Regions of the V<sub>2</sub>T with phosphorylated residues pS357, pT359, pT360 (<bold>b)</bold> and pS362, pS363, and pS364 (<bold>c</bold>) interacting with positively charged residues on βarr1, which are colored in green and labeled in blue.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="nihms-1541120-f0004.jpg"><?image-name nihms-1541120-f0004.jpg?><?image-size 388938?><?image-md5 650b402b9f2ecf554f66285c622e1935?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 3001?><?image-original-width 2891?><?image-scaled-height 750?><?image-scaled-width 722?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/650b402b9f2e/nihms-1541120-f0004.jpg?><?thumb-name nihms-1541120-f0004.gif?><?thumb-size 3686?><?thumb-md5 4534096e54ae0ab75dd607533692ae75?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 103?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/4534096e54ae/nihms-1541120-f0004.gif?></graphic></fig><fig id="F5" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Fig. 5.</label><caption><title>Comparison of the megaplex βarr1–V<sub>2</sub>T to the V<sub>2</sub>Rpp–βarr1–Fab30 and rhodopsin–visual arrestin crystal structures.</title><p id="P79"><bold>a</bold>, Superimposition between the βarr1–V<sub>2</sub>T (megaplex) and the V<sub>2</sub>Rpp–βarr1–Fab30 crystal structure. Inset shows interaction between pT347 and pS350 of the V<sub>2</sub>Rpp with residues near the finger loop region of βarr1. Red and yellow arrows delineate the differing path of the V<sub>2</sub>T and V<sub>2</sub>Rpp, respectively. <bold>b</bold>, Alignment between βarr1–V<sub>2</sub>T (megaplex) and the rhodopsin–visual arrestin crystal structure. For clarity, only βarr1 of the megaplex is shown in <bold>a</bold> and <bold>b</bold>.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="nihms-1541120-f0005.jpg"><?image-name nihms-1541120-f0005.jpg?><?image-size 357189?><?image-md5 926e161b77766fdee9c5c54a41db91e2?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 3397?><?image-original-width 4200?><?image-scaled-height 646?><?image-scaled-width 800?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/926e161b7776/nihms-1541120-f0005.jpg?><?thumb-name nihms-1541120-f0005.gif?><?thumb-size 2840?><?thumb-md5 2b80aed8a727c76fc4bc9de246050052?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 80?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/2b80aed8a727/nihms-1541120-f0005.gif?></graphic></fig><fig id="F6" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 6.</label><caption><title>The megaplex within a membrane environment.</title><p id="P80"><bold>a</bold>, Orthogonal views of three modeled megaplex structures for molecular dynamics (MD) simulations with differing βarr1–V<sub>2</sub>T positions, aligned by their β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–Gs region. <bold>b</bold>, Coarse-grained MD models of the three structures, aligned by their β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–Gs region at 10 ns increments. Black circles denote transient contact between βarr1 residues and the lipid bilayer. <bold>c</bold>, Real-time cAMP measurement of BI-stimulated β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R or β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–βarr1/2 in β<sub>2</sub>AR/βarr1/βarr2 triple knock-out HEK293 cells, expressed as percentages of 10 μM Forskolin control. Data represents mean ± S.E.M. of four independent experiments for the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–βarr1 and β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–βarr2 conditions and of three independent experiments for the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R condition. Ordinary one-way ANOVA with Holm-Sidak’s multiple comparison post-hoc test was performed to determine statistical significance between mock control and the β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R or β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–βarr1/2 conditions (****<italic toggle="yes">P</italic> &lt; 0.0001).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" position="float" orientation="portrait" xlink:href="nihms-1541120-f0006.jpg"><?image-name nihms-1541120-f0006.jpg?><?image-size 639071?><?image-md5 c3cf2232adc700a85bb88a52c41afa79?><?image-image-server-status LOAD_COMPLETED?><?image-original-height 1857?><?image-original-width 2100?><?image-scaled-height 707?><?image-scaled-width 800?><?image-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/c3cf2232adc7/nihms-1541120-f0006.jpg?><?thumb-name nihms-1541120-f0006.gif?><?thumb-size 4032?><?thumb-md5 3182a6a4daad87b3f2e11c78f113c109?><?thumb-image-server-status NEVER_LOAD?><?thumb-scaled-height 88?><?thumb-scaled-width 100?><?thumb-cloudpmc-urn urn:cdn:blobs/31cb/7108872/3182a6a4daad/nihms-1541120-f0006.gif?></graphic></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float" orientation="portrait"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p id="P81">Cryo-EM data collection, refinement and validation statistics</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/><col align="left" valign="middle" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Megaplex Consensus Reconstruction (EMD-9377)</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">β<sub>2</sub>V<sub>2</sub>R–Gs (EMD-9376, PDB 6NI3)</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">βarr1–V<sub>2</sub>T (EMD-9375, PDB 6NI2)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Data collection and processing</bold></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Magnification</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">105,000</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">105,000